WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.325
Deduplication, and which copy wins.

2
00:00:02.605 --> 00:00:12.141
Enterprise corpora are full of copies: the same policy in the wiki and the shared drive, a contract and its signed scan, a deck and its PDF export.

3
00:00:12.421 --> 00:00:14.533
Duplicates hurt retrieval directly.

4
00:00:14.813 --> 00:00:24.264
If five copies of one paragraph are the most similar chunks to a question, they fill all five top-k slots and crowd out the second source the answer needed.

5
00:00:24.544 --> 00:00:31.392
They also make conflicts invisible: two near-identical copies that disagree on one number look like agreement.

6
00:00:31.672 --> 00:00:35.064
Research on training data shows how common this is at scale.

7
00:00:35.344 --> 00:00:52.709
Lee and colleagues (2021) found a single 61-word sentence repeated over 60,000 times in the C4 dataset, and reported that models trained on deduplicated data emitted memorized text about ten times less often.

8
00:00:52.989 --> 00:01:00.435
Retrieval corpora are smaller, but the same lesson applies: you will not know how much duplication you have until you measure it.

9
00:01:00.715 --> 00:01:02.336
Two levels catch most of it.

10
00:01:02.616 --> 00:01:08.803
Exact duplicates: hash the cleaned text (SHA-256) and keep one copy per hash.

11
00:01:09.083 --> 00:01:21.584
Near duplicates: break each document into word shingles (overlapping runs of about five words), and compare sets with the Jaccard similarity, the size of the intersection over the size of the union.

12
00:01:21.864 --> 00:01:23.763
Pairs above a threshold (0.

13
00:01:24.043 --> 00:01:28.501
8 is a reasonable start, then inspect what it catches) are near copies.

14
00:01:28.781 --> 00:01:44.589
Comparing every pair is quadratic, so at scale use MinHash with locality-sensitive hashing, which estimates Jaccard similarity from small signatures and finds candidate pairs without comparing everything; the datasketch library implements it.

15
00:01:44.869 --> 00:01:58.523
Then decide, and write down, which copy wins: the newest by date, the one from the system of record, or the one with the most permissive or most restrictive access (careful: merging copies with different permissions can leak).

16
00:01:58.803 --> 00:02:06.696
When two near copies disagree on content, consider keeping both and flagging the conflict for an owner rather than silently picking one.
